{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01GacxeB5rz7AXM2EJufWYVF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q3: QLoRA vs LoRA — when choose one?**\n\n---\n\n**`answers/04-003-qlora-vs-lora-when-choose-one.md`** covers:\n\n- **Concept** — LoRA keeps the frozen base in BF16; QLoRA quantizes it to 4-bit NF4, cutting base model VRAM by 4×\n- **Mechanism** — NF4 quantization (optimal for normally-distributed weights), double quantization of scale constants (FP32→FP8), paged optimizers for Adam state offloading to CPU RAM; adapter matrices stay in BF16 throughout\n- **Memory table** — 7B/13B/70B VRAM comparison: QLoRA halves or quarters the requirement vs LoRA\n- **Code example** — `BitsAndBytesConfig` + `LoraConfig` for Llama 3 8B fitting on an RTX 3090 (~9 GB)\n- **Cost comparison** — Llama 3 13B legal summarization: LoRA costs ~$80 (2× A100), QLoRA ~$20 (1× A100), ROUGE-L 0.81 vs 0.79\n- **Key pitfalls**: confusing *what* gets quantized (frozen base only, not adapters), treating QLoRA as always inferior without benchmarking, and conflating QLoRA (training-time) with GPTQ/AWQ (inference-time)\n- **Decision rule**: VRAM > 40 GB → LoRA; VRAM < 16 GB → QLoRA required; in between depends on model size"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":354,"cache_read_input_tokens":68880,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":354,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d8f8cccc-d6f1-4156-b9b7-6b005f70ec39","uuid":"bed42a7a-687d-47a9-9b75-507f5d6a4469"}
